\paragraph*{The \jet component}
\begin{figure}
\rotatebox{0}{\resizebox{8.6cm}{!}{
	\includegraphics{TrigPt.eps}
}}
\vspace{-.8cm}
\caption{Dependence of \jet yield on \pttrig.  The yield is nearly independent of collision system and is consistent with the \dAu.  The shape of the dependence on \pttrig is well described by \PT.  Color online.}\label{Figure1}
\end{figure}


The dependence of the \jet yield on \pttrig for \stdassoc is plotted in \Fref{Figure1}.  The yield increases constantly with \pttrig for both \Cu and \Au and for \sNNsixtytwo and \sNNtwohundred.  There is no difference between \Cu and \Au collisions.  This is consistent with the \jet component arising from fragmentation.  The yield at \sNNsixtytwo is considerable lower than that at \sNNtwohundred.  This is expected if the \jet component comes from fragmentation since, for a fixed \pttrig, the momentum fraction carried by the trigger \zTdef should be higher in \sNNsixtytwo due to the more steeply falling jet spectrum.



\begin{figure}
\rotatebox{0}{\resizebox{8.6cm}{!}{
	\includegraphics{AssocPt.eps}
}}
\vspace{-.8cm}
\caption{Dependence of \jet yield on \ptassoc.  The key is the same as in \fref{Figure1}.  The yield is nearly independent of collision system and is consistent with the \dAu.  The spectrum from \PT is slightly harder, which may explain why the \pttrig dependence must be scaled.  Color online.}\label{Figure2}
\end{figure}

