The spectra of particles associated with the \jet for \stdtrig are shown in \fref{Figure2}.  As expected, the spectra are softer for \sNNsixtytwo.  The inverse slope parameters from an exponential fit to these data are shown in \tref{Table}.    There is no difference seen between \Cu and \Au in either the data points or the extracted inverse slope parameter.


\begin{figure}
\rotatebox{0}{\resizebox{8.6cm}{!}{
	\includegraphics{JetNpart.eps}
}}
\vspace{-.8cm}
\caption{Dependence of \jet yield on \npart.  The \jet yield in \Cu and \Au are within error at both \sNNsixtytwo and \sNNtwohundred.  There is a slight increase with \npart.  This may be attributed to the method.  If the \ridge is not completely independent of \deta it would contribute more to the yield at high \npart.  The yield at \sNNsixtytwo is about 40\% lower than at \sNNtwohundred.  This would be expected if the \jet were primarily from fragmentation.  Color online.}\label{Figure3}
\end{figure}


\begin{figure}
\rotatebox{0}{\resizebox{8.6cm}{!}{
	\includegraphics{Widths.eps}
}}
\vspace{-.8cm}
\caption{Dependence of \jet yield on \npart.  The \jet yield in \Cu and \Au are within error at both \sNNsixtytwo and \sNNtwohundred.  There is a slight increase with \npart.  This may be attributed to the method.  If the \ridge is not completely independent of \deta it would contribute more to the yield at high \npart.  The yield at \sNNsixtytwo is about 40\% lower than at \sNNtwohundred.  This would be expected if the \jet were primarily from fragmentation.  Color online.}\label{Width}
\end{figure}

The \npart dependence of the \jet yield is shown in \fref{Figure3}.  For a given \npart and \sNN there is no difference between \Cu and \Au.  The \jet yield shows a slight increase at higher \npart, which would occur if the \ridge were not completely independent of \deta and therefore were not completely subtracted.  

%\textit{This section needs to be fleshed out.  Needs nice pretty \PT comparisons, which means discussing how to make the \PT comparisons.}
The ratio of the yield at \sNNsixtytwo to that at \sNNtwohundred can be explained by \PT.  This combined with the independence of the \jet on the system implies that the dominant production mechanism for the \jet is vacuum fragmentation.  The slight increase of the \jet yield with \npart implies that perhaps there is either a slight modification of the fragmentation function or that the \ridge is not completely independent of \deta and was not completely subtracted.

\begin{figure}
\rotatebox{0}{\resizebox{8.6cm}{!}{
	\includegraphics{PythiaJet.eps}
	\includegraphics{PythiazT.eps}
}}
\vspace{-.8cm}
\caption{\zT distribution at \sNNsixtytwo and \sNNtwohundred as predicted by \PT for \stdtrig and \stdassoc.}\label{Figure4}
\end{figure}



Since \PT explains the \jet component reasonably well and the spectra associated with the \jet at \sNNsixtytwo falls more steeply than at \sNNtwohundred, the differences in \zT at the two energies are probably qualitatively similar to those from \PT.  The \zT distribution from \PT for both collision energies is shown in \fref{Figure4}.  Since we expect the medium producted at RHIC to have similar properties for different collision energies, comparing jet-like correlations at two different collision energies is a way of comparing different jet energies.

\paragraph*{The \ridge component}

\begin{figure}
\rotatebox{0}{\resizebox{8.6cm}{!}{
	\includegraphics{RidgeNpart.eps}
}}
\vspace{-.8cm}
\caption{Dependence of \ridge yield on \npart.  The \ridge yield at \sNNsixtytwo is about 40\% lower than at \sNNtwohundred.  Color online.}\label{Figure5}
\end{figure}

This is one way to test the dependence of the \ridge on jet energy.  The dependence of the \ridge yield in \npart is shown in \fref{Figure5} for both systems and energies.  There is no difference within errors between \Cu and \Au collisions; the \ridge yield is consistent with system-size dependence.  The yield increases sharply with \npart, but is considerably smaller for \sNNsixtytwo than for \sNNtwohundred, just like the \jet yield.


\begin{figure}
\rotatebox{0}{\resizebox{8.6cm}{!}{
	\includegraphics{RidgeRatio.eps}
}}
\vspace{-.8cm}
\caption{Dependence of \ridge/\jet on \npart.  This ratio is independent of energy within errors, at least in the kinematic range investigated.  Color online.}\label{Figure6}
\end{figure}
This motivates investigating the \ridge/\jet ratio, shown in \fref{Figure6}.  This ratio is independent of collision energy.


\section{Discussion}
%\textit{This section will be filled in with the comparison of theories, inasmuch as possible.  Need to write theorists to get figures to compare to, get some feel for whether or not this is consistent with the theory or not.  Right now these data can be explained by the momentum-kick model.}
\section{Conclusions}
The \jet yield shows no dependence on collision system, and the dependence on collision energy can be explained well by \PT.  This implies that the dominant mechanism for the formation of the \jet component of the \nearside of \highpT correlations is fragmentation, although there is room within the errors for small contributions from other mechanisms.

The study of correlations at different collision energies gives a way to study different jet energies.  The \ridge is considerably smaller at \sNNsixtytwo than at \sNNtwohundred, but the ratio of the \ridge/\jet is found to be independent of \sNN within the kinematic range studied.

